[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"CSS Custom Properties (Variables) and Scope Architecture"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS Custom Properties, commonly referred to as CSS variables, offer a powerful way to store and reuse specific values throughout a stylesheet. Unlike preprocessor variables, CSS variables are dynamic, live in the DOM, and respect cascade and scope architecture."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Scope Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because CSS custom properties are tied to the DOM tree, they follow standard CSS cascade rules. This means they inherit values from parent elements and can be overridden locally within specific component scopes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":":root {
  --primary-color: #3498db;
  --spacing-unit: 1rem;
}

.card {
  --primary-color: #e74c3c;
  background-color: var(--primary-color);
  padding: var(--spacing-unit);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Global Scope: Declared on the :root pseudo-class, making variables accessible across the entire document."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Local Scope: Declared within specific selectors, overriding global definitions for that element and its descendants."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Fallback Values and Robustness"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can provide a fallback value as the second argument in the var() function. This ensures that if a custom property is not defined, the browser falls back to a safe default."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":".element {
  color: var(--text-color, #333333);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Dynamic Styling with JavaScript"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the greatest architectural benefits of CSS custom properties is their interaction with JavaScript. You can read and write variable values on the fly, making theme switching extremely performant."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--primary-color', '#2ecc71');"}]
